n\n2) require solving costly optimization programs to compute\n\nweight vectors. In this paper, we introduce a relatively simple\n\napproach based on bilinear programming that naturally in-\n\ncludes the broadband case while not being signiﬁcantly more\n\ncomputationally intensive than broadband beamforming with\n\na known direction of arrival θ.\n\nRecent work in [2] has shown how broadband beamforming\n\ncan effectively be treated as a linear inverse problem from\n\nmeasurements y≈A(θ)α that can be solved with least\n\nsquares (this is discussed in more detail in Section II-A).\n\nThe forward model A(θ)of course depends on the angle of\n\narrival (AOA) θ. In this paper, we show how we can make\n\nthis broadband beamforming method robust to uncertainty in\n\nEmail: nsingh360@gatech.edu, cdelude3@gatech.edu, mdav@gatech.edu,\n\njrom@ece.gatech.edu. This work was supported in part by COGNISENSE,\n\none of seven centers in JUMP 2.0, a Semiconductor Research Corporation\napproximated by vectors from a low dimensional subspace.\n\nThat is, there exists a K×P matrix U ℓ, for a very small P,\n\nsuch that for every ﬁxed θ∈Θ we have a ℓ(θ)≈U ℓ c for a\n\nshort vector c∈C P. As such, we can write y ℓ=c H U H\n\nℓ α\n\nwhere both c and α are unknown — α depends on the signal\n\ns(t)and c depends on its AOA.\n\nWe formalize this carefully as follows. Deﬁne the functions\n\nw ℓ,k(θ) = e−j 2 π f c τ m(θ)ψ k(t n−τ m(θ)), θ ∈Θ,\n\nfor an interval Θ as above, and collect these into\n\na ℓ(θ) = \n\n\n\n\n\nw ℓ,1(θ)\n\n.\n\n.\n\n.\n\nw ℓ,K (θ)\n\n\n\n\n\n.\n\nOur problem is that we observe\n\ny ℓ=a ℓ(θ∗)H α+ noise, ℓ = 1, . . . , M N, (5)\n\nfor some unknown θ∗∈Θ. We will show how this can be\n\nturned into a well-posed bilinear problem by using some basic\n\nfacts about reproducing kernel Hilbert spaces.\n\nLet H be the Hilbert space of functions on θ∈Θ generated\n\nfrom the functions w ℓ,k(θ)\n\nH= Span({w ℓ,k(θ), ℓ = 1,...,MN;k= 1,...K}).(6)\n\nThis is a ﬁnite-dimensional Hilbert space of continuous\n\nfunctions, and thus it will have a reproducing kernel for any\n\ninner product h·,·i that we endow it with. H is essentially a\n\nSlepian space, as it is generated from modulated versions of\n\nsmall segments of the (approximately) bandlimited functions\n\nψ 1(t),...,ψ K(t).\n\nLet W ℓ:H → C K be the linear operator deﬁned by\n\nW ℓ x=\n\n\n\n\n\nh x, w ℓ,1 i\n\n.\n\n.\n\n.\n\nh x, w ℓ,K i\n\n\n\n\n\n(7)\n\nand W:H → C M N K be the concatenation of these operators\n\nacross the array elements and snapshots:\n\nW x=\n\nc:\n\nˆ\n\nc t+2 =arg min\n\nc\n\n||¯\n\ny−V t+1 U\n\n|{z }\n\nA t+1\n\nc\n\nc||2\n\n2\n\n=⇒ˆ\n\nc t+2 = ((A t+1\n\nc)H A t+1\n\nc)−1(A t+1\n\nc)H¯\n\ny.\n\nIt is important to note that we solve an unconstrained bilinear\n\nleast squares problem. Therefore, the optimizers of the pro-\n\ngram are not unique, i.e., if the algorithm recovers weights ˆ\n\nα,\n\nˆ\n\nc then ˆ\n\nα/γ and γ ˆ\n\nc are also feasible solutions of the problem\n\nfor γ 6= 0. To ﬁnd γ associated with the true angle, we can\n\ncreate a sequence of weights c q for each θ q sampled from\n\nΘ. For a sufﬁciently ﬁne-grained sampling, the true c will be\n\nwell-approximated by some c∈ {c 1,...,c Q}. Thus, if we\n\nlet\n\nq∗=arg min\n\nqc q−γ q ˆ\n\nc\n\n2\n\n2\n\nwhere γ q=c T\n\nq ˆ\n\nc\n\nˆ\n\nc T ˆ\n\nc, then γ q∗is the offset constant linked\n\nwith the true angle and ˆ\n\nα/γ q∗provides the estimate of the\n\ntrue Slepian weights. Other approaches, like line spectral\n\nestimation [4], can also be adapted for ﬁne-tuning the constant\n\nγ. We empirically observe that using a sufﬁciently good\n\ninitialization for c 0 results in γ≈1. Thus, we start the\n\nalternating minimization scheme described above with an\n\ninitial value of c 0 corresponding to the mid-point of the\n\ninterval Θ, allowing the algorithm to converge in 10 to 15\n\niterations for the experiments illustrated in the following\n\nsection.\n\nIV. N UM ERI CA L RES ULT S\n\nWe use the linear Slepian model in (4) to perform two\n\nkinds of beamforming to benchmark the performance of the\n\nproposed model. In the ﬁrst scenario, we assume that the\n\nangle of arrival is known and true Slepian weights (α true)\n\nare evaluated using (4). In the second case, we use the\n\nmidpoint of the uncertainty interval (Θ) as an estimate for\n\nMVDR-RAB performs as well as the proposed method with\n\nonly a marginal drop in performance; however, extending it\n\nto broadband is not trivial due to several drawbacks inherent\n\nin the optimization problem setup. As seen from (9), the\n\nnorm constraint makes the problem non-convex, requiring\n\nSDP-relaxation to ﬁnd an optimal solution. In the case of\n\nbroadband, it is difﬁcult to implement SDP relaxation due to\n\nthe matrices involved. Also, the objective function might not\n\nremain convex when extended to broadband since there is no\n\nstraightforward analog of the steering vector for broadband. In\n\ncontrast, the proposed bilinear algorithm is simple and works\n\nregardless of our operating regime.\n\nFig. 4.\n\nNominal SNR compared to Beamformed SNR for a 16\n\n×\n\n16\n\nelement UPA with 32 snapshots in the broadband regime with band-\n\nwidth\n\n5 GHz\n\n.\n\nFig. 5.\n\nNominal SNR compared to Beamformed SNR for a 64-element\n\nULA with 32 snapshots in the narrowband regime with bandwidth\n\n5 MHz\n\n.\n\nR EF ERE NC ES\n\n[1] A. Elbir, K. Mishra, S. Vorobyov, and R. Heath, “Twenty-Five Years of\n\nAdvances in Beamforming: From convex and nonconvex optimization\n\nto learning techniques,” IEEE Signal Process. Mag., vol. 40, no. 4, p.\n\n118–131, 2023.\n\n[2] C. DeLude, M. Davenport, and J. Romberg, “Slepian Beam-\n\nforming: Broadband Beamforming using Streaming Least Squares,”\n\narXiv:2312.03922, December 2023.\n\n[3] D. Slepian, “On bandwidth,” Proceedings of the IEEE, vol. 64, no. 3,\n\npp. 292–300, 1976.\n\n5\n\n[4] C. DeLude, R. Sharma, S. Karnik, C. Hood, M. Davenport, and\n\nJ. Rom