arrival is known to only be within an interval of reasonable size, there is very little loss in estimation performance compared to the case where the angle is known exactly.\n\n* 6 Pages, 5 figures  \n\nFull-text Links:View paper on--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nColeman DeLude (https://www.catalyzex.com/author/Coleman%20DeLude)\nciteturn214search6 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 4 months ago; Image: Figure 1 for Robust Broadband Beamforming using Bilinear Programming ... Via Image: arxiv icon\n\n## Robust Broadband Beamforming using Bilinear Programming\n\nRequest Code\n\nImage: Bookmark button\n\nImage: Alert button\n\nJun 24, 2024\n\nAuthors:Nakul Singh, Coleman DeLude, Mark A. Davenport, Justin Romberg\n\nFigures and Tables:\n\nImage: Figure 1 for Robust Broadband Beamforming using Bilinear Programming\n\nImage: Figure 2 for Robust Broadband Beamforming using Bilinear Programming\n\nImage: Figure 3 for Robust Broadband Beamforming using Bilinear Programming\n\nImage: Figure 4 for Robust Broadband Beamforming using Bilinear Programming\n\nAbstract:We introduce a new method for robust beamforming, where the goal is to estimate a signal from array samples when there is uncertainty in the angle of arrival. Our method offers state-of-the-art performance on narrowband signals and is naturally applied to broadband signals. Our beamformer operates by treating the forward model for the array samples as unknown. We show that the \"true\" forward model lies in the linear span of a small number of fixed linear systems. As a result, we can estimate the forward operator and the signal simultaneously by solving a bilinear inverse problem using least squares. Our numerical experiments show that if the angle of arrival is known to only be within an interval of reasonable size, there is very little loss in estimation performance compared to the case where the angle is known exactly.\n\n* 6 Pages, 5 figures \n\nViaImage: arxiv icon\n\nAccess Paper or Ask Questions\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nau:Davenport_M in:eess - SciRate Search (https://scirate.com/search?q=au%3ADavenport_M+in%3Aeess)\nciteturn214search7 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 2 months ago;   * Robust Broadband Beamforming using Bilinear Programming ... ~Davenport and Justin Romberg}, title = {{R}obust {B}roadband {B}eamforming using {B}ilinear {P}rogramming}, year = {2024}, eprint = {2406.16304}, note = {arXiv:2406.16304v1} }[Button: Copy Citation]\nOct 21 2024 cs.LG eess.SP arXiv:2410.14667v3\n\n[Button: Scited][Button: Scite!]\n\n0\n\n@misc{2410.14667, author = {Peimeng Guan and Mark A.~Davenport}, title = {{SGD} {J}ittering: {A} {T}raining {S}trategy for {R}obust and {A}ccurate {M}odel-{B}ased {A}rchitectures}, year = {2024}, eprint = {2410.14667}, note = {arXiv:2410.14667v3} }[Button: Copy Citation]\n\nPDF\n\nInverse problems aim to reconstruct unseen data from corrupted or perturbed measurements. While most work focuses on improving reconstruction quality, generalization accuracy and robustness are equally important, especially for safety-critical applications. Model-based architectures (MBAs), such as loop unrolling methods, are considered more interpretable and achieve better reconstructions. Empirical evidence suggests that MBAs are more robust to perturbations than black-box solvers, but the accuracy-robustness tradeoff in MBAs remains underexplored. In this work, we propose a simple yet effective training scheme for MBAs, called SGD jittering, which injects noise iteration-wise during reconstruction. We theoretically demonstrate that SGD jittering not only generalizes better than the standard mean squared error training but is also more robust to average-case attacks. We validate SGD jittering using denoising toy examples, seismic deconvolution, and single-coil MRI reconstruction. Both SGD jittering and its SPGD extension yield cleaner reconstructions for out-of-distribution data and demonstrates enhanced robustness against adversarial attacks.\n\n  * Robust Broadband Beamforming using Bilinear Programming\n\nNakul Singh, Coleman DeLude, Mark A. Davenport, Justin Romberg\n\nJun 25 2024 eess.SP arXiv:2406.16304v1\n\n[Button: Scited][Button: Scite!]\n\n0\n\n@misc{2406.16304, author = {Nakul Singh and Coleman DeLude and Mark A.~Davenport and Justin Romberg}, title = {{R}obust {B}roadband {B}eamforming using {B}ilinear {P}rogramming}, year = {2024}, eprint = {2406.16304}, note = {arXiv:2406.16304v1} }[Button: Copy Citation]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nau:Romberg_J in:eess - SciRate Search (https://scirate.com/search?q=au%3ARomberg_J+in%3Aeess)\nciteturn214search8 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: last month;   * Robust Broadband Beamforming using Bilinear Programming ... ~Davenport and Justin Romberg}, title = {{R}obust {B}roadband {B}eamforming using {B}ilinear {P}rogramming}, year = {2024}, eprint = {2406.16304}, note = {arXiv:2406.16304v1} }[Button: Copy Citation]\n  * Robust Broadband Beamforming using Bilinear Programming\n\nNakul Singh, Coleman DeLude, Mark A. Davenport, Justin Romberg\n\nJun 25 2024 eess.SP arXiv:2406.16304v1\n\n[Button: Scited][Button: Scite!]\n\n0\n\n@misc{2406.16304, author = {Nakul Singh and Coleman DeLude and Mark A.~Davenport and Justin Romberg}, title = {{R}obust {B}roadband {B}eamforming using {B}ilinear {P}rogramming}, year = {2024}, eprint = {2406.16304}, note = {arXiv:2406.16304v1} }[Button: Copy Citation]\n\nPDF\n\nWe introduce a new method for robust beamforming, where the goal is to estimate a signal from array samples when there is uncertainty in the angle of arrival. Our method offers state-of-the-art performance on narrowband signals and is naturally applied to broadband signals. Our beamformer operates by treating the forward model for the array samples as unknown. We show that the \"true\" forward model lies in the linear span of a small number of fixed linear systems. --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nKatalog Plus der Bibliothek der Frankfurt UAS: Artikel & mehr... (https://hds.hebis.de/fuas/EBSCO/Search?filter%5B%5D=addfacetfilter%28Publisher%3Aieee%29&lookfor=Davenport%2C+Mark&type=author)\nciteturn214search9 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 4 months ago; Robust Broadband Beamforming using Bilinear Programming\n\nIhre Suchbegriffe : Einfache Suche: (Verfasser: Davenport, Mark) , Suchdauer: 0.66s\nIhre Suchfilter : Verlag: ieee\n\n  * Bücher & mehr\n  * Artikel & mehr (124)\n\n# Volltext-Artikel, E-Books und Literaturhinweise aus dem EBSCO Discovery Service\n\n#### Ergebnis verändern\n\nSuchfilter entfernen\n\n[Input] [Input] [Input] [Input]\n\neinschränken\n    [Input] Volltextzugriff\n    [Input] Academic (peer-reviewed) journals\n    [Input]\nerweitern\n    [Input] Auch Volltexte der Artikel durchsuchen\n    [Input] Verwandte Themen suchen\n\n[Input]\n\nTreffer 1 - 25 von 124, Suchdauer: 0.66s\n\nSortieren [Select] [Input]\n\n12345weiter »\n\n  * 1\n\nA Fast Broadband Beamspace Transformation\n\nvon Singh, Nakul ; DeLude, Coleman ; Davenport, Mark A. ; et al.\n\n2025 IEEE 10th International Workshop on Computational Advances in Multi-Sensor Adaptive Processing (CAMSAP) Computational Advances in Multi-Sensor Adaptive Processing (CAMSAP), 2025 IEEE 10th International Workshop on. :56-60 Dec, 2025\n\n    * Zum Volltext in IEEE Xplore\n\nImage: Kein Bild verfügbar\n\nMerkliste\n\n  *   * 2\n\nRobust Broadband Beamforming using Bilinear Programming\n\nvon Singh, Nakul ; DeLude, Coleman ; Davenport, Mark A. ; et al.\n\n2024 International Workshop on the Theory of Computational Sensing and its Applications to Radar, Multimodal Sensing and Imaging (CoSeRa) Theory of Computational Sensing and its Applications to Radar, Multimodal Sensing and Imaging (CoSeRa), 2024 International Workshop on the. :106-111 Sep, 2024\n\n    * Zum Volltext in IEEE Xplore\n\nImage: Kein Bild v